Heap pollution is subtle because the type violation and the crash happen in different places. Consider a method that takes generic varargs and stores them unsafely.

```java
// DANGEROUS — stores into the varargs array
static <T> T[] toArray(T... args) {
    return args; // returns Object[] disguised as T[]
}

static <T> T[] pickTwo(T a, T b, T c) {
    return toArray(a, b); // compiler creates Object[]{a, b}
}

String[] result = pickTwo("a", "b", "c"); // ClassCastException!
```

The compiler creates `Object[]{"a", "b"}` for the `toArray` call because `T` erases to `Object`. The method returns this `Object[]`. When the caller assigns it to `String[]`, the JVM inserts a checkcast that fails — you're assigning `Object[]` to `String[]`. The confusing part is that the `ClassCastException` is at the caller, not inside `toArray`.

`@SafeVarargs` is appropriate **only when the method reads from the array but never writes to it or returns it**. Methods like `Collections.addAll()` and `List.of()` are safe — they iterate over elements but don't expose the underlying array. If your method stores into the array, passes it to another method, or returns it, the annotation is a lie.

The swap method is the simplest proof because it depends entirely on whether the caller's variables can be redirected from inside a method. In Java, they can't.

```java
static void swap(String a, String b) {
    String temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}

String x = "first", y = "second";
swap(x, y);
System.out.println(x + ", " + y); // "first, second" — unchanged
```

The method receives **copies** of the references. Reassigning those copies has no effect on the caller's `x` and `y`. In a true pass-by-reference language like C++ (with `&` parameters) or C# (with `ref`), the swap would succeed because the method would receive the caller's actual variable slots, not copies.

The same principle explains why `sb = new StringBuilder("Replaced")` inside a method doesn't affect the caller, while `sb.append(" World")` does. Reassignment changes the local copy. Mutation changes the shared heap object. **Both behaviors are consistent with pass-by-value** — the difference is whether you're redirecting your copy of the pointer or using your copy to reach the shared object.

Two rules make a correct HashMap key: implement the contract, and never mutate after insertion.

## Use Records — The Compiler Gets It Right

```java
// Before: manual, error-prone — easy to forget hashCode or get equals wrong
public class UserId {
    private final String tenant;
    private final long id;

    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) { /* 8 lines, easy to mess up */ }
    @Override public int hashCode() { /* easy to forget entirely */ }
}

// After: record generates equals() + hashCode() from all fields automatically
record UserId(String tenant, long id) {}

Map<UserId, User> cache = new HashMap<>();
cache.put(new UserId("acme", 42), user);
cache.get(new UserId("acme", 42));  // always works — consistent hash
```

Records are **shallow-immutable by default** — their fields are `final`, assigned at construction, and cannot change. This satisfies both rules at once: correct contract + effective immutability.

## Never Mutate a Key After Insertion

```java
List<String> key = new ArrayList<>(List.of("a", "b"));
map.put(key, "value");

key.add("c");  // hashCode() of the list just changed

map.get(key);  // computes new hash → different bucket → returns null
               // entry is permanently stranded in the old bucket
               // only way to recover: clear and rebuild the map
```

If you must use a mutable object as a key, **snapshot it at insertion time** — create an immutable copy and use that as the key, not the live object.

A record declaration like `record Point(int x, int y) {}` generates six things: a `final` class, `private final` fields, a canonical constructor, accessor methods `x()` and `y()` (not `getX()`), `equals()`, `hashCode()`, and `toString()`.

The **compact constructor** is where validation belongs. It omits the parameter list and the field assignments — those happen automatically after the constructor body.

```java
record Email(String value) {
    Email { // compact constructor — no parentheses
        if (!value.contains("@")) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid email");
        value = value.toLowerCase(); // reassignment to parameter, not field
    }
}
```

The `value = value.toLowerCase()` line modifies the parameter before the auto-generated `this.value = value` assignment. This is the correct way to normalize data in records — you can't assign to `this.value` directly in a compact constructor.

You can override any generated method — accessors, `equals()`, `toString()` — but doing so should be rare. The moment you override `equals()` to exclude a field, you break the semantic contract that records represent. If you need that flexibility, use a regular class.

Java 21 added **record patterns** in `switch` expressions: `case Point(int x, int y) when x > 0` deconstructs the record and applies a guard — making records first-class citizens in pattern matching.

Records allow customization within boundaries. You can add **static fields and methods**, **instance methods**, and **override** any generated method. You can also add **constructors** — but every constructor must delegate to the canonical constructor.

The **compact constructor** is the recommended customization point for validation and normalization. It has no parameter list — the parameters are implicit, and the field assignments happen automatically after the body executes.

```java
record Range(int min, int max) {
    Range { // compact — validates before auto-assignment
        if (min > max) throw new IllegalArgumentException("min > max");
    }
}
```

You can override `equals()`, `hashCode()`, or `toString()`, but doing so carries risk. If you exclude a component from `equals()`, two records with different field values could be considered equal — breaking the **semantic transparency** that records guarantee. The compiler won't stop you, but tools and frameworks that rely on records being value-based will behave unexpectedly.

Accessors can be overridden to return **defensive copies**. If a record holds a mutable object like `List`, returning the original reference lets callers mutate it. Overriding `items()` to return `Collections.unmodifiableList(items)` preserves immutability — though the better practice is storing an unmodifiable copy in the compact constructor.

The combination of sealed interfaces, records, and pattern matching replaces the **visitor pattern** — one of the most verbose design patterns in Java — with concise, compiler-checked code.

Consider modeling arithmetic expressions. The traditional approach uses an `Expression` interface with an `accept(Visitor)` method, concrete classes implementing it, and a `Visitor` interface with `visit()` methods for each type. Adding a new expression type requires modifying the visitor interface and all implementations.

With sealed classes and records, the same model is compact and the compiler enforces completeness.

```java
sealed interface Expr permits Num, Add, Mul {
}
record Num(double value) implements Expr {}
record Add(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr {}
record Mul(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr {}

double eval(Expr e) {
    return switch (e) {
        case Num(var v) -> v;
        case Add(var l, var r) -> eval(l) + eval(r);
        case Mul(var l, var r) -> eval(l) * eval(r);
    }; // no default — compiler knows this is exhaustive
}
```

The `switch` expression deconstructs each record variant using pattern matching. If you add `record Sub(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr {}` to the `permits` list, every `switch` over `Expr` will produce a compile error until you handle `Sub`. This is the compile-time safety that the visitor pattern tried to achieve through runtime `visit()` dispatch.

**`WeakHashMap`** is the most commonly used weak reference structure. Its keys are weak references — when a key has no strong references remaining, the entry is automatically removed from the map on the next GC cycle. This is useful for **canonicalization maps** and metadata caches where entries should disappear when the key object is no longer used elsewhere.

```java
WeakHashMap<Object, String> metadata = new WeakHashMap<>();
Object key = new Object();
metadata.put(key, "attached data");
key = null; // after next GC, the entry is automatically removed
```

The danger is using `WeakHashMap` as a general cache — objects get collected too aggressively. For caching, `SoftReference` is more appropriate because soft references survive until memory pressure forces collection.

**`ReferenceQueue`** enables cleanup callbacks. When the GC clears a reference (soft, weak, or phantom), it enqueues the Reference object into an associated `ReferenceQueue`. A background thread can poll this queue and perform cleanup — closing native handles, releasing off-heap memory, or logging diagnostics.

Java 9's **`Cleaner`** API wraps this pattern cleanly, replacing the deprecated `finalize()` method. You register a cleaning action with `Cleaner.register(obj, cleanupRunnable)`, and when `obj` becomes phantom-reachable, the runnable executes on a dedicated cleaner thread. This is the modern way to clean up native resources without the unpredictability of finalizers.
